{"vid":"xwseWCSXD3Y","source":"xwseWCSXD3Y.en-GB.json3","lang":"en","cues":[{"t":7.9,"txt":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil."},{"t":11.8,"txt":"And I'm Georgie."},{"t":13,"txt":"In English, there are many ways to describe smells."},{"t":16,"txt":"Poet Michael Rosen wanted to find words describing bad smells,"},{"t":19.8,"txt":"and asked listeners of his BBC Radio 4 programme Word of Mouth"},{"t":23.9,"txt":"for some suggestions."},{"t":25.4,"txt":"Fusty, reeking, toiking, honking, humming, funky."},{"t":28.7,"txt":"Smells are everywhere,"},{"t":30,"txt":"whether they're bad smells like rotten eggs and sweaty feet,"},{"t":33.8,"txt":"or pleasant ones like lavender and freshly baked bread."},{"t":37.8,"txt":"What smells do you especially like or dislike, Georgie?"},{"t":41.6,"txt":"Well, I really enjoy the smell of leather,"},{"t":44.8,"txt":"but something I really love is the smell of rain, which has a name."},{"t":49.6,"txt":"Do you know it, Neil?"},{"t":50.6,"txt":"Erm, I don't think I do actually."},{"t":53.3,"txt":"It's called petrichor."},{"t":54.7,"txt":"It's that smell of the ground just after it's rained."},{"t":57.4,"txt":"Mmm."},{"t":58,"txt":"Neil, what about you?"},{"t":59.1,"txt":"Which smells do you like?"},{"t":60.5,"txt":"Well, I don't think you can beat the smell of a freshly opened packet"},{"t":64.9,"txt":"of ground coffee."},{"t":66.1,"txt":"Ooh, that's a good one."},{"t":67.5,"txt":"In this episode, we'll be learning the language of smells using"},{"t":70.6,"txt":"some useful new words and phrases."},{"t":72.5,"txt":"And remember, you can find all the vocabulary plus a quiz and worksheet"},{"t":76.6,"txt":"on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."},{"t":79.5,"txt":"But first, I have a question for you, Georgie."},{"t":82.3,"txt":"One smell many people love is the smell of old books"},{"t":85.9,"txt":"and the sentimental feeling it gives them for second-hand bookshops."},{"t":89.8,"txt":"In fact, there's even a word for it, but what is it?"},{"t":93.4,"txt":"Is it: a) vellichor, b) petrichor, or c) nidor?"},{"t":98.6,"txt":"Well, we already know that petrichor is the smell of the ground after rain."},{"t":104,"txt":"I'm going to go with a) vellichor."},{"t":106.5,"txt":"Well, we'll find out at the end of the programme."},{"t":109.7,"txt":"Listening to Michael Rosen's list – funky, reeking, humming and the rest –"},{"t":114.4,"txt":"you might think English is good at describing smells,"},{"t":117.7,"txt":"but in fact, all those words really mean is bad smelling."},{"t":122,"txt":"In English, we often describe smells by comparing them to other objects."},{"t":126.5,"txt":"For example, something nice might smell like a rose,"},{"t":129.9,"txt":"while something bad might smell like rotten eggs,"},{"t":132.8,"txt":"but there are few words to describe the actual smell itself."},{"t":137.2,"txt":"Oxford University psychologist Asifa Majid has researched the subject"},{"t":141.5,"txt":"of smell extensively."},{"t":143.2,"txt":"Here, she tells Michael Rosen a little of what she's found out"},{"t":146.8,"txt":"on BBC Radio 4 programme Word of Mouth."},{"t":149.6,"txt":"So, if we think about things like stinky or fragrant,"},{"t":153.3,"txt":"they're definitely telling us that something is good or bad."},{"t":156.3,"txt":"But unlike colour terms,"},{"t":158.1,"txt":"it doesn't really seem to be about the object it's describing."},{"t":161,"txt":"It seems to be a subjective thing."},{"t":163.1,"txt":"I could say, oh this perfume you think is really fragrant,"},{"t":167.6,"txt":"I think is stinky, and both can be true."},{"t":170.6,"txt":"Describing smell is not like describing other senses."},{"t":174,"txt":"I might think a perfume is stinky, an adjective meaning bad smelling,"},{"t":178.5,"txt":"while Neil might find the same perfume fragrant, meaning pleasant smelling."},{"t":183.3,"txt":"Both can be true."},{"t":184.7,"txt":"The point is that neither stinky nor fragrant say anything specific"},{"t":188.6,"txt":"about the perfume's actual smell."},{"t":190.8,"txt":"With our sense of taste, it's different."},{"t":193.4,"txt":"Words like spicy, sweet or salty create a common idea that we can all agree on,"},{"t":199,"txt":"but that's not the case for smells."},{"t":201.2,"txt":"Asifa thinks this is because,"},{"t":203,"txt":"unlike tastes and colours, smell words are subjective –"},{"t":206.6,"txt":"they're based on personal opinions and feelings rather than objective truth."},{"t":211.3,"txt":"This might explain why our attitude to talking about smells is so different."},{"t":215.9,"txt":"Here's Asifa again, sharing some examples of how we talk"},{"t":219.5,"txt":"about smells with BBC Radio 4."},{"t":222.1,"txt":"So, if we compare how often people talk about visual things,"},{"t":226,"txt":"or auditory things, or tastes or smells, what we find is we talk a lot"},{"t":230.8,"txt":"about what we see and very rarely about what we smell."},{"t":234.3,"txt":"Young children in the UK are often interested in the sense of smell"},{"t":239.5,"txt":"and they'll remark on it, and the parent will shut it down."},{"t":242.7,"txt":"They'll say, that's disgusting!"},{"t":244.6,"txt":"Put it down."},{"t":246,"txt":"Asifa's research shows that, here in the UK at least, people talk"},{"t":249.8,"txt":"about visual or auditory things much more than they talk about smells."},{"t":254,"txt":"The adjective 'visual' means related to seeing,"},{"t":257.2,"txt":"and 'auditory' means related to hearing."},{"t":259.8,"txt":"The adjective for things related to smelling is 'olfactory'."},{"t":263.9,"txt":"Maybe this is because of how we're raised by our parents."},{"t":267.1,"txt":"When children start talking about smells, especially unpleasant ones,"},{"t":271.1,"txt":"parents often react by shutting the conversation down."},{"t":274.8,"txt":"The phrase 'to shut something down' means to stop it."},{"t":277.9,"txt":"Along with other European languages,"},{"t":280.1,"txt":"English doesn't have much vocabulary dedicated to the different qualities"},{"t":284.1,"txt":"of actual smells."},{"t":285.4,"txt":"There are many ways of talking about smells, especially unpleasant ones,"},{"t":289.3,"txt":"but only a few words which describe actual smells themselves."},{"t":293.4,"txt":"A good example of a word which does this is 'musty' – a damp, stale smell"},{"t":298.4,"txt":"you might find in an old bookshop."},{"t":300.6,"txt":"And Neil, that reminds me of your question."},{"t":303.9,"txt":"That's right."},{"t":304.5,"txt":"I asked you what the word is, which means the smell of old books."},{"t":309.3,"txt":"I said it was vellichor."},{"t":311.7,"txt":"Well, Georgie, you are absolutely right."},{"t":314.6,"txt":"Yay!"},{"t":315.4,"txt":"Petrichor, as you said earlier, is the smell of the earth after rain."},{"t":319.5,"txt":"And nidor is the smell of cooking meat and fat."},{"t":323.6,"txt":"OK. It's time for a recap of the vocabulary we've learned."},{"t":326.8,"txt":"The adjective 'stinky' means foul smelling,"},{"t":330.2,"txt":"while the adjective 'fragrant' means pleasant smelling."},{"t":333.1,"txt":"Subjective things are based on your own personal opinions and feelings"},{"t":337.4,"txt":"rather than objective facts."},{"t":339.5,"txt":"The adjective 'visual' describes things related to seeing,"},{"t":343,"txt":"while 'auditory' means related to hearing."},{"t":345.5,"txt":"Things related to smelling are called 'olfactory'."},{"t":349,"txt":"And finally, the phrasal verb 'to shut something down' means to stop it."},{"t":353.2,"txt":"Once again, our six minutes are up,"},{"t":355.1,"txt":"but there's still time to head over to our website, bbclearningenglish.com,"},{"t":359.9,"txt":"for a quiz and worksheet containing all the vocabulary we've discussed."},{"t":364,"txt":"See you again soon!"},{"t":365.1,"txt":"But for now, it's goodbye."},{"t":366.7,"txt":"Goodbye!"}],"words":[{"t":7.88,"w":"Hello,"},{"t":7.88,"w":"this"},{"t":7.88,"w":